[{"page": 1, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-1", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "T5. Cardiolipins", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 150.9783477783203, 179.86404418945312, 172.68597412109375]}, {"text": "6%", "bbox": [290.6499938964844, 193.6215057373047, 304.6280822753906, 207.18873596191406]}, {"text": "5.1", "bbox": [188.49600219726562, 209.5625, 202.5609893798828, 223.12973022460938]}, {"text": "5.2", "bbox": [220.1219940185547, 209.5625, 234.18698120117188, 223.12973022460938]}, {"text": "5.3", "bbox": [251.7480010986328, 209.5625, 265.8129577636719, 223.12973022460938]}, {"text": "5.4", "bbox": [283.3739929199219, 209.5625, 297.43896484375, 223.12973022460938]}, {"text": "5.5", "bbox": [315.0, 209.5625, 329.0649719238281, 223.12973022460938]}, {"text": "5.6", "bbox": [346.6260070800781, 209.5625, 360.69097900390625, 223.12973022460938]}, {"text": "Σ", "bbox": [389.4079895019531, 194.64697265625, 395.6246643066406, 229.23712158203125]}, {"text": "1", "bbox": [192.67999267578125, 225.50250244140625, 198.3763885498047, 239.06973266601562]}, {"text": "6", "bbox": [224.30599975585938, 225.50250244140625, 230.0023956298828, 239.06973266601562]}, {"text": "4", "bbox": [255.9320068359375, 225.50250244140625, 261.6283874511719, 239.06973266601562]}, {"text": "2", "bbox": [287.5579833984375, 225.50250244140625, 293.2543640136719, 239.06973266601562]}, {"text": "2", "bbox": [319.18402099609375, 225.50250244140625, 324.8804016113281, 239.06973266601562]}, {"text": "3", "bbox": [350.80999755859375, 225.50250244140625, 356.5063781738281, 239.06973266601562]}, {"text": "18", "bbox": [386.8190002441406, 225.50250244140625, 398.2139892578125, 239.06973266601562]}, {"text": "In this problem you are asked to be creative and build molecules from fragments. For instance, if you", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 259.33349609375, 538.5557861328125, 272.9007263183594]}, {"text": "are required to draw a chiral phospholipid such as W using fragments a-e, it can be assembled as shown", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 271.28851318359375, 538.5786743164062, 284.8557434082031]}, {"text": "below.", "bbox": [56.692962646484375, 283.2434997558594, 87.24891662597656, 296.81072998046875]}, {"text": "Cardiolipins are a family of acyclic phospholipids differing in fatty acid residues. They are essential for", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 457.3445129394531, 538.5650024414062, 470.9117431640625]}, {"text": "energy production and membrane stability. Defects can lead to certain diseases. PL1 belongs to this", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 469.3005065917969, 538.5733032226562, 482.86773681640625]}, {"text": "family. Using only the structural elements a-d in the quantities stated below, it is possible to assemble", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 481.2554931640625, 538.5802001953125, 494.8227233886719]}, {"text": "the non-ionised form of PL1. R is a hydrocarbon substituent in the fatty acid structure.", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 493.2104797363281, 459.5451965332031, 506.7777099609375]}, {"text": "PL1 does not contain any peroxide bonds.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 600.1644897460938, 252.990478515625, 613.731689453125]}]}, {"page": 2, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-2", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "5.1", "bbox": [78.60800170898438, 166.2294921875, 92.84233856201172, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Tick one correct statement regarding the value of n (number of type a frag-", "bbox": [114.69253540039062, 166.2294921875, 480.5858154296875, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "ments).", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 178.18450927734375, 149.80982971191406, 191.75173950195312]}, {"text": "(a) n is an even number,", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 190.1405029296875, 226.56520080566406, 203.70773315429688]}, {"text": "(b) n is an odd number,", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 202.0955047607422, 223.02951049804688, 215.66273498535156]}, {"text": "(c) n can be either an odd or an even number.", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 214.05050659179688, 327.4753112792969, 227.61773681640625]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 226.00550842285156, 169.74588012695312, 239.57273864746094]}, {"text": "Each bond involves two atoms, so the total number of atoms with broken bonds", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 237.96051025390625, 480.5575866699219, 251.52774047851562]}, {"text": "(indicated by the wavy line on the fragments of the molecule) must be an even", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 249.91552734375, 480.5890197753906, 263.4827575683594]}, {"text": "number. For fragments with a known number of repeats in the PL1 molecule,", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 261.87152099609375, 480.5864562988281, 275.4387512207031]}, {"text": "the total number of atoms with broken bonds is 17 (2·2 + 3·3 + 4). Thus the", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 273.8265075683594, 480.58709716796875, 287.39373779296875]}, {"text": "total number of hydrogen atoms with broken bonds must be an odd number.", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 285.781494140625, 480.5382995605469, 299.3487243652344]}, {"text": "Therefore, n is an odd number.", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 297.73651123046875, 259.6454772949219, 311.3037414550781]}, {"text": "Correct answer: 1 point.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 358.4425048828125, 227.36868286132812, 372.0097351074219]}, {"text": "1.0 pt", "bbox": [495.45001220703125, 166.15451049804688, 521.8194580078125, 179.72174072265625]}, {"text": "Some cardiolipins are chiral molecules, even if they contain four identical fatty acid residues, as in the case", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 388.1335144042969, 538.5836181640625, 401.70074462890625]}, {"text": "of PL1. One enantiomer of PL1 is found in prokaryotes and eukaryotes, and the other only in archaea. All", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 400.0885009765625, 538.5548095703125, 413.6557312011719]}, {"text": "other diastereomers of PL1 are achiral molecules, since they have a plane of symmetry. Do not consider", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 412.0434875488281, 538.5767822265625, 425.6107177734375]}, {"text": "the chirality of phosphorus atoms as stereocentres.", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 423.9994812011719, 296.97161865234375, 437.56671142578125]}, {"text": "PL1 is a diprotic acid with the same acidic groups.", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 441.9314880371094, 312.6356201171875, 455.49871826171875]}, {"text": "Its second deprotonation is less favourable", "bbox": [324.11187744140625, 441.9314880371094, 538.576416015625, 455.49871826171875]}, {"text": "(𝑝𝐾𝑎2 ≫𝑝𝐾𝑎1) due to the special, stable structure of monoanion Y.", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 438.9719543457031, 370.16864013671875, 476.0520935058594]}]}, {"page": 3, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-3", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "5.2", "bbox": [78.60800170898438, 166.2294921875, 92.84233856201172, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Draw the structure of one enantiomer of PL1. Draw the structure of Y in a way", "bbox": [114.69253540039062, 166.2294921875, 480.580078125, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "that shows the stabilisation that explains the difference between 𝑝𝐾𝑎1 and", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 163.26898193359375, 461.4952697753906, 199.44757080078125]}, {"text": "𝑝𝐾𝑎2. Use the abbreviation R for the fatty acid residues.", "bbox": [114.6910400390625, 175.2249755859375, 373.289306640625, 211.40257263183594]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 202.0955047607422, 169.74588012695312, 215.66273498535156]}, {"text": "To obtain the diastereomer of PL1 with a plane of symmetry, we must construct", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 214.05050659179688, 480.5755920410156, 227.61773681640625]}, {"text": "a symmetric structure from the given fragments. Glycerol backbones can only", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 226.00550842285156, 480.56524658203125, 239.57273864746094]}, {"text": "be linked through phosphate groups since there is no peroxide bond present.", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 237.96051025390625, 480.55816650390625, 251.52774047851562]}, {"text": "The number of glycerol moieties suggests that one glycerol occupies the central", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 249.91552734375, 480.5855712890625, 263.4827575683594]}, {"text": "position with the remaining fragments arranged around it to achieve an overall", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 261.87152099609375, 480.5755920410156, 275.4387512207031]}, {"text": "plane of symmetry. The number of a fragments in PL1 is n = 3·3 – 2·2 – 4 = 1.", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 273.8265075683594, 480.5932922363281, 287.39373779296875]}, {"text": "So, PL1 has 𝑑2𝑐−𝑏−𝑐(𝑎) −𝑏−𝑐𝑑2 skeleton. Two options, presented below, can", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 270.865966796875, 480.5692443847656, 307.0445861816406]}, {"text": "have a plane of symmetry:", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 297.73651123046875, 238.1382598876953, 311.3037414550781]}, {"text": "The PL0 structure is achiral, so it cannot be PL1. All possible stereoisomers of", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 404.9844970703125, 480.579833984375, 418.5517272949219]}, {"text": "PL1 are as follows:", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 416.9394836425781, 201.42079162597656, 430.5067138671875]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 428.8945007324219, 169.74588012695312, 442.46173095703125]}, {"text": "Enantiomer 2 is found in prokaryotes and eukaryotes. These organisms use", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 564.5974731445312, 480.56134033203125, 578.1646728515625]}, {"text": "glycerol 3-phosphate for cardiolipin synthesis, while archaea use glycerol 1-", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 576.552490234375, 480.58526611328125, 590.1196899414062]}, {"text": "phosphate for this purposes and synthesise enantiomer 1:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 588.5075073242188, 383.82861328125, 602.07470703125]}, {"text": "6.0 pt", "bbox": [495.45001220703125, 166.15451049804688, 521.8194580078125, 179.72174072265625]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 618.198486328125, 111.74788665771484, 631.7656860351562]}]}, {"page": 4, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-4", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "PL1", "bbox": [288.8480224609375, 337.96051025390625, 306.4297790527344, 351.5277404785156]}, {"text": "For drawing of enantiomer 1 or 2 as PL1: 4 points.", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 452.33349609375, 300.5267639160156, 465.9007263183594]}, {"text": "For drawing the correct structure of PL1 with wrong or missing stereochemistry: 3 points.", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 464.2884826660156, 485.1436767578125, 477.855712890625]}, {"text": "For drawing achiral PL0 structure: 2 points.", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 476.24346923828125, 268.1390380859375, 489.8106994628906]}, {"text": "Any other structure that uses exactly 1×a, 2×b, 3×c, 4×d and contains no peroxide bond: 1 point.", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 488.1984558105469, 513.3333740234375, 501.76568603515625]}, {"text": "Intramolecular hydrogen bond between the phosphoric acid residue and the alcohol group signifi-", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 510.11651611328125, 529.1771240234375, 523.6837158203125]}, {"text": "cantly reduces the 𝑝𝐾𝑎2 value.", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 507.15594482421875, 206.26280212402344, 543.3345336914062]}, {"text": "For any correct tautomer of Y: 2 points.", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 603.4285278320312, 249.65200805664062, 616.9957275390625]}, {"text": "For a tautomer of Y (not shown above) with a hydrogen bond between the two phosphate groups:", "bbox": [66.05799865722656, 615.383544921875, 529.1925659179688, 628.9507446289062]}, {"text": "1 point", "bbox": [66.0580062866211, 627.3385620117188, 98.81343841552734, 640.90576171875]}, {"text": "Correctly drawn structures with incorrect PL1 structure are also accepted.", "bbox": [66.0580062866211, 639.2935791015625, 410.0598449707031, 652.8607788085938]}, {"text": "For structures containing hydrogen bond between phosphoric acid residues and esters 0 point, be-", "bbox": [66.05802917480469, 651.2485961914062, 529.1773071289062, 664.8157958984375]}, {"text": "cause they are possible for each phosphoric acid residue even in molecular form and don’t make", "bbox": [66.05802917480469, 663.20361328125, 529.1990356445312, 676.7708129882812]}, {"text": "difference between 𝑝𝐾𝑎1 and 𝑝𝐾𝑎2.", "bbox": [66.05802917480469, 660.2440795898438, 228.79066467285156, 696.421630859375]}, {"text": "A series of experiments was conducted to identify the fatty acid (RCOOH) in PL1 found predominantly", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 705.0374755859375, 538.5782470703125, 718.6046752929688]}, {"text": "in mammalian hearts. During reductive ozonolysis, RCOOH forms three different organic products in", "bbox": [56.693023681640625, 716.9924926757812, 538.565673828125, 730.5596923828125]}, {"text": "equimolar amounts.", "bbox": [56.693023681640625, 728.947509765625, 151.64723205566406, 742.5147094726562]}]}, {"page": 5, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-5", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "5.3", "bbox": [78.60800170898438, 166.2294921875, 92.84233856201172, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Determine the molecular formula of the fatty acid (RCOOH), if the non-ionised", "bbox": [114.69253540039062, 166.2294921875, 480.5820617675781, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "form of PL1 contains 255 𝜎and 𝜋bonds between atoms in total. Support your", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 163.26898193359375, 480.5856018066406, 197.859130859375]}, {"text": "answer with calculations.", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 192.43150329589844, 231.93154907226562, 205.9987335205078]}, {"text": "If you were unable to find the structural formula of PL1, you can use a-d fragments", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 204.38650512695312, 480.57257080078125, 217.9537353515625]}, {"text": "from 5.1.", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 216.3415069580078, 155.88462829589844, 229.9087371826172]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 228.2965087890625, 169.74588012695312, 241.86373901367188]}, {"text": "Correct scheme of reductive ozonolysis of RCOOH is as follows:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 240.2515106201172, 408.63818359375, 253.81874084472656]}, {"text": "So, 𝑁𝐶=𝐶= 2.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 314.24298095703125, 179.2661590576172, 350.42156982421875]}, {"text": "If we take R as 𝐶𝑥𝐻𝑦, then:", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 326.1979675292969, 239.6811065673828, 362.3765563964844]}, {"text": "y=(2x+1)-𝑁𝐶=𝐶∙2=2x-3.(eq.3.1)", "bbox": [114.69098663330078, 338.1529541015625, 256.6602783203125, 374.33154296875]}, {"text": "Then the formula for calculating the number of bonds in PL1 can be written as", "bbox": [114.69098663330078, 376.9794616699219, 480.5794372558594, 390.54669189453125]}, {"text": "follows:", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 388.9344482421875, 150.4972381591797, 402.5016784667969]}, {"text": "0.5∙1+5∙2+11.5∙3+4∙(3+2x+0.5y)=255,", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 400.8894348144531, 283.4481506347656, 414.4566650390625]}, {"text": "4x+y=99.(eq.3.2)", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 412.84442138671875, 190.663818359375, 426.4116516113281]}, {"text": "Combining eq.3.1 and eq.3.2:", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 424.7994079589844, 251.3087921142578, 438.36663818359375]}, {"text": "4x+y=4x+2x-3=6x-3=99 ⟹x=17, y=31.", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 421.8398742675781, 294.6286926269531, 456.4300231933594]}, {"text": "RCOOH −C17H31COOH or C18H32O2", "bbox": [114.69097900390625, 445.7498474121094, 295.36474609375, 480.3399963378906]}, {"text": "For correct eq.3.2: 2 points.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 472.6205139160156, 242.9202880859375, 486.187744140625]}, {"text": "For correct number of double bonds: 1 point.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 484.57550048828125, 326.21759033203125, 498.1427307128906]}, {"text": "For correct formula of C17H31COOH: 1 point.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 496.5304870605469, 325.9328918457031, 512.7118530273438]}, {"text": "4.0 pt", "bbox": [495.45001220703125, 166.15451049804688, 521.8194580078125, 179.72174072265625]}, {"text": "100 g of RCOOH reacts with 181.0 g of iodine. RCOOH also reacts in similar way with X and forms an", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 526.8214721679688, 538.5682983398438, 540.388671875]}, {"text": "adduct with an iodine mass fraction of 36.57%.", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 538.7775268554688, 274.635498046875, 552.3447265625]}]}, {"page": 6, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-6", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "5.4", "bbox": [78.60800170898438, 166.2294921875, 92.84233856201172, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Determine the molecular formula of X. Support your answer with calculations.", "bbox": [114.69253540039062, 166.2294921875, 480.5780334472656, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 180.1304931640625, 169.74588012695312, 193.69772338867188]}, {"text": "During determination of the iodine number, the following reaction takes place:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 192.0854949951172, 480.5680847167969, 205.65272521972656]}, {"text": "The iodine number of RCOOH and the iodine mass fraction in the adduct are", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 256.3894958496094, 480.56719970703125, 269.95672607421875]}, {"text": "described as follows:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 268.344482421875, 211.8867950439453, 281.9117126464844]}, {"text": "181.0 = 𝑁C=C×253.8", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 263.2912292480469, 200.23489379882812, 301.0561218261719]}, {"text": "𝑀RCOOH", "bbox": [156.51998901367188, 273.8052062988281, 193.88333129882812, 304.9365539550781]}, {"text": "× 100 ⇒𝑀RCOOH", "bbox": [203.6439971923828, 263.2912292480469, 286.0323486328125, 301.05615234375]}, {"text": "𝑁C=C", "bbox": [255.76800537109375, 273.8052062988281, 278.9298095703125, 306.2759094238281]}, {"text": "= 140.2", "bbox": [290.489013671875, 266.46600341796875, 322.4888610839844, 301.05615234375]}, {"text": "0.3657 =", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 286.4289855957031, 151.6721954345703, 321.0191345214844]}, {"text": "𝑁C=C×126.9", "bbox": [194.5900115966797, 283.2542419433594, 244.1628875732422, 315.7249450683594]}, {"text": "𝑀RCOOH+𝑁C=C×(126.9+𝐴Hal)", "bbox": [155.6439971923828, 295.1712341308594, 283.1092834472656, 328.1045837402344]}, {"text": "⇒𝐴Hal = 79.9 g mol−1.", "bbox": [289.8399963378906, 285.0002136230469, 398.5716247558594, 323.8460998535156]}, {"text": "So, X is iodine monobromide, IBr.", "bbox": [114.69094848632812, 319.36846923828125, 271.487548828125, 332.9356994628906]}, {"text": "If X was molecular iodine (I2), then:", "bbox": [114.69094848632812, 341.2864685058594, 279.9438171386719, 357.0528259277344]}, {"text": "𝜔𝐼=", "bbox": [114.69094848632812, 341.7579345703125, 136.67633056640625, 377.9365234375]}, {"text": "𝑁C=C×253.8", "bbox": [162.63894653320312, 338.58319091796875, 212.2118377685547, 371.05389404296875]}, {"text": "𝑀RCOOH+𝑁C=C×253.8 = 0.6441", "bbox": [140.63999938964844, 341.7579650878906, 275.1551513671875, 381.5669250488281]}, {"text": "which is incorrect.", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 372.34149169921875, 199.04502868652344, 385.9087219238281]}, {"text": "For correct formula of X: 1 point.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 384.2965087890625, 267.7169189453125, 397.8637390136719]}, {"text": "For correct calculations: 1 point.", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 396.2514953613281, 264.8280334472656, 409.8187255859375]}, {"text": "2.0 pt", "bbox": [495.45001220703125, 166.15451049804688, 521.8194580078125, 179.72174072265625]}, {"text": "The phase behavior of PL1 depends on pH. In physiological conditions, PL1 is a dianion and it forms a", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 425.9425048828125, 538.567626953125, 439.5097351074219]}, {"text": "lamellar phase.", "bbox": [56.693023681640625, 437.8974914550781, 128.1454620361328, 451.4647216796875]}]}, {"page": 7, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-7", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "5.5", "bbox": [78.60800170898438, 166.2294921875, 92.84233856201172, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Which lipid phase is formed by PL1 when both acid residues in it are protonated?", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 166.2294921875, 480.57696533203125, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Note that knowledge of PL1 structure is not required to solve this question.", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 178.18450927734375, 466.9203186035156, 191.75173950195312]}, {"text": "Tick the correct answer.", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 190.1405029296875, 227.62091064453125, 203.70773315429688]}, {"text": "(a) micellar", "bbox": [141.16500854492188, 358.30450439453125, 192.443359375, 371.8717346191406]}, {"text": "(b) lamellar", "bbox": [244.3179931640625, 358.30450439453125, 296.94134521484375, 371.8717346191406]}, {"text": "(c) hexagonal", "bbox": [343.281005859375, 358.30450439453125, 405.6350402832031, 371.8717346191406]}, {"text": "(d) inverse", "bbox": [453.9840087890625, 352.3265075683594, 502.58123779296875, 365.89373779296875]}, {"text": "hexagonal", "bbox": [453.7799987792969, 364.281494140625, 502.7940979003906, 377.8487243652344]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 391.73651123046875, 169.74588012695312, 405.3037414550781]}, {"text": "PL1 has 4 acyl chains but only 2 phosphate head groups, so its tail volume is", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 403.6914978027344, 480.57672119140625, 417.25872802734375]}, {"text": "already large relative to the head group — which is why the dianion is lamellar,", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 415.646484375, 480.578125, 429.2137145996094]}, {"text": "not micellar/hexagonal. Protonating both acidic groups shrinks the head group", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 427.6014709472656, 480.57000732421875, 441.168701171875]}, {"text": "further (loss of charge and its water shell), tipping the balance even more to-", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 439.5574645996094, 480.5652770996094, 453.12469482421875]}, {"text": "ward the tails. Starting from lamellar, this shift leads to the inverse hexagonal", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 451.512451171875, 480.5856018066406, 465.0796813964844]}, {"text": "phase. Correct answer is (d).", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 463.4674377441406, 247.483154296875, 477.03466796875]}, {"text": "Only for correct answer: 2 point.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 475.4225158691406, 266.1829528808594, 488.98974609375]}, {"text": "2.0 pt", "bbox": [495.45001220703125, 166.15451049804688, 521.8194580078125, 179.72174072265625]}]}, {"page": 8, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-8", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "The following balanced reaction equations show the hydrolysis of phospholipids PL1-PL3:", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 155.21949768066406, 474.5105895996094, 168.78672790527344]}, {"text": "- The non-ionised form of PL2 contains 254 𝜎and 𝜋bonds between atoms in total. It is a chiral and a", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 308.5619812011719, 538.5755615234375, 343.1521301269531]}, {"text": "more symmetrical molecule than PL1,", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 335.4335021972656, 233.75363159179688, 349.000732421875]}, {"text": "- Z contains C, H, and O only, and it doesn’t react with H2 in the presence of a catalyst,", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 353.3655090332031, 456.3771667480469, 369.4237365722656]}, {"text": "- Phospholipid PL3 is a charged molecule at physiological pH values, and it exists as pair of enantiomers.", "bbox": [56.6929931640625, 371.29852294921875, 538.578369140625, 384.8657531738281]}]}, {"page": 9, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-9", "bbox": [410.3869934082031, 59.77293014526367, 538.61376953125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8269958496094, 128.718505859375, 535.9927978515625, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "5.6", "bbox": [78.60800170898438, 166.2294921875, 92.84233856201172, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Draw the structures of PL2 and PL3 using R to show the fatty acid residues.", "bbox": [114.69253540039062, 166.2294921875, 480.581298828125, 179.79672241210938]}, {"text": "Show stereochemistry where appropriate.", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 178.18450927734375, 312.1720275878906, 191.75173950195312]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 190.1405029296875, 169.74588012695312, 203.70773315429688]}, {"text": "The hydrolysis of PL1 proceeds through breaking of 8 ester bonds, which re-", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 202.0955047607422, 480.5652770996094, 215.66273498535156]}, {"text": "quires 8 water molecules. So, PL2 also contains all these ester bonds and has", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 214.05050659179688, 480.5780029296875, 227.61773681640625]}, {"text": "the same skeleton with PL1 and the residue of molecule Z needs to be located", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 226.00550842285156, 480.5796203613281, 239.57273864746094]}, {"text": "in the centre of PL2 due to its symmetry:", "bbox": [114.69100952148438, 237.96051025390625, 304.7770690917969, 251.52774047851562]}, {"text": "In the case of Z reducing the number of covalent bonds, leads to decreasing", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 341.2695007324219, 480.5720520019531, 354.83673095703125]}, {"text": "numbers of C, H or O atoms:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 353.2254943847656, 248.1705780029297, 366.792724609375]}, {"text": "- removing each C atom decreases the number of covalent bonds by 3;", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 365.18048095703125, 443.9456481933594, 378.7477111816406]}, {"text": "- removing a pair of H atoms forms a C=C or C=O double bond, which reacts", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 377.1354675292969, 480.5775146484375, 390.70269775390625]}, {"text": "with 𝐻2 in presence of catalyst;", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 374.1749267578125, 260.5993347167969, 410.353515625]}, {"text": "- removing each O atom decreases the number of covalent bonds by 1.", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 401.0454406738281, 445.4300842285156, 414.6126708984375]}, {"text": "Thus, Z is propane-1,3-diol: it contains only C, H, and O; does not react with", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 413.00042724609375, 480.5816650390625, 426.5676574707031]}, {"text": "H2 in the presence of a catalyst; has one fewer covalent bond; and is more", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 424.9564208984375, 480.571533203125, 441.0136413574219]}, {"text": "symmetrical than glycerol.", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 436.9114074707031, 238.15818786621094, 450.4786376953125]}, {"text": "Note that replacing OH with H makes the PL2 molecule more symmetrical – a", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 448.86639404296875, 480.5895690917969, 462.4336242675781]}, {"text": "𝐶2 rotation axis appears:", "bbox": [114.69099426269531, 445.9058532714844, 230.50440979003906, 482.0844421386719]}, {"text": "This diastereomer of these enantiomers isn’t chiral due to its symmetry plane.", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 687.6204833984375, 480.57818603515625, 701.1876831054688]}, {"text": "PL3 needs to be synthesised from the same precursors and via the same", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 699.5755004882812, 480.5682373046875, 713.1427001953125]}, {"text": "metabolic pathways as PL1. So, it can be one of the following enantiomers:", "bbox": [114.69100189208984, 711.530517578125, 464.8067321777344, 725.0977172851562]}, {"text": "3.0 pt", "bbox": [495.45001220703125, 166.15451049804688, 521.8194580078125, 179.72174072265625]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 741.2214965820312, 111.74788665771484, 754.7886962890625]}]}, {"page": 10, "width": 595.280029296875, "height": 841.8900146484375, "mode": "native", "lines": [{"text": "Theory", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 63.1240119934082, 105.85767364501953, 82.42530822753906]}, {"text": "Q5-10", "bbox": [377.97100830078125, 59.77293014526367, 538.6260986328125, 136.97825622558594]}, {"text": "English (Official)", "bbox": [460.8270263671875, 128.718505859375, 535.992919921875, 142.28573608398438]}, {"text": "The right structure is typical of prokaryotes and eukaryotes, while the left one is possible for archaea.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 228.11050415039062, 538.5746459960938, 241.677734375]}, {"text": "PL3 demonstrates the structure of phosphatidic acid derivatives such as phosphatidylcholine.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 240.0655059814453, 493.0492858886719, 253.6327362060547]}, {"text": "SOLUTION:", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 257.9985046386719, 111.74788665771484, 271.56573486328125]}, {"text": "For correct structure of PL2 with correct stereochemistry: 2 points.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 626.6744995117188, 368.4169921875, 640.24169921875]}, {"text": "For correct structure of PL2 with incorrect stereochemistry: 1 point.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 644.6074829101562, 372.37213134765625, 658.1746826171875]}, {"text": "For the structure of PL2 with a keto group in the central position: 1 point.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 662.5394897460938, 399.20941162109375, 676.106689453125]}, {"text": "For correct structure of PL3 without any stereochemistry: 1 point.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 680.4724731445312, 363.30621337890625, 694.0396728515625]}, {"text": "For structure of PL3 with monoethyl phosphate in central position: 1 point", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 698.4054565429688, 404.2408752441406, 711.97265625]}, {"text": "Correctly drawn structures corresponding to PL1 in answer to 5.2 are also accepted.", "bbox": [56.69300079345703, 716.3384399414062, 448.5838317871094, 729.9056396484375]}]}]